55,3,1,talk,Minato,,"Candidate A sounds like it helps with notes, but Candidate C solves the copying problem Midori found. I worry A might add more to the coordinators.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
56,3,1,talk,Jonathan,,Candidate D is out because of the network and approval issues I found. Candidate A and C both depend on developers who are currently unavailable.,"{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
57,3,1,talk,George,,"Candidate B is a production unit, likely fixed-fee work, which fits our budget. Candidate C might involve expensive per-head licenses if it's a vendor tool.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
58,3,1,talk,May,,"Candidate A requires daily use by many, which my research says usually fails. Candidate C is a hub, but if it's just a catalog, it might survive.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
59,3,1,talk,Midori,,Candidate C directly addresses the misrouted deliveries and the hour lost to copying records. It solves a real problem on the floor.,"{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
60,3,2,talk,Minato,,But @Jonathan said developers are busy. Can Candidate C be built without them? Or maybe Candidate B is safer for the staff?,"{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,2,talk,Jonathan,,"Candidate B is the safest regarding technical dependencies. It's content production, not software development. Candidate C is risky if the interfaces are undocumented.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,2,talk,George,,"I agree with Jonathan. Candidate B avoids the ""per-head"" license trap and the frozen capital budget. It's a sustainable investment.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,2,talk,May,,"Candidate B is a ""narrow tool"" for skill transfer, which fits the pattern of success I found. It doesn't require everyone to log in daily.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,2,talk,Midori,,"While B is safe, C solves the most urgent pain. However, if it's impossible to implement due to developers, it won't help anyone.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,3,talk,Minato,,"I feel bad for the people copying records, but I feel worse for the coordinators. Candidate B seems like it wouldn't break the rota.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,3,talk,Jonathan,,I cannot support D or C given the technical roadblocks. B is the only one that doesn't rely on unstable network or unavailable developers.,"{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,3,talk,George,,I'm leaning toward B. It's financially prudent and avoids the renewal surprises I'm concerned about.,"{""words"":15,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,3,talk,May,,I'll vote for B. It's the most likely to actually be used and survive its first year based on the historical data.,"{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,3,talk,Midori,,"If the others feel B is the only viable option despite the pain C would solve, I will support the harmony of the group.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,4,talk,Minato,,I agree. Let's go with B.,"{""words"":6,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
